  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": null,
      "what": "An analysis of over 3,000 cities globally reveals that built environmental mass scales sublinearly with population both across cities (beta = 0.90) and across neighborhoods within a given city (delta = 0.75). The mathematical framework shows that city-level scaling efficiencies directly originate from the internal, uneven spatial distribution of residents.",
      "why": "This challenges the widespread assumption that building material requirements scale linearly with population growth, demonstrating that larger urban areas and denser neighborhoods naturally achieve higher material efficiency per capita."
